<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:54:01Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Ugandan royal succession scandal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6e374fb5b812ad82fe50d959ada85832814d52fe832a1ddcdcc99bde31998810</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:53:07Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T20:20:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal--dc969309a6f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, a dramatic struggle for control of a throne in Uganda. Also, Rwanda shut down 101 alcoholic beverage factories last month and declared a public health crisis after at least 50 people were killed by contaminated illegal supplies. And Ethiopia celebrates its new year.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Infantino is the one who has brought the most to Africa' says Samuel Eto'o</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e688fa32aab37305b95b8afbb2328620af7d90f156d2960070644304e18aa20b</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:53:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T14:31:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o--38a56194e182.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, former football star and current president of the Cameroonian Football Federation Samuel Eto'o spoke about the recent backlash towards FIFA president Gianni Infantino and confirmed he will support him for re-election because of "everything he has brought to African football", adding that the African federations will defend what seems "right to us given the challenges that we face in our own federations".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Algeria cuts diplomatic ties with United Arab Emirates over 'provocative and hostile actions'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37eeec9672f2263dbd1520ca79f854fbd758bed0e760d4c643fc57e820db2ad5</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:53:58Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T11:50:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions--dd9c4cc89c40.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions" /><summary type="html">Algeria is severing diplomatic ties with the United Arab Emirates, state media reported Thursday. Algerian President ‌Abdelmadjid Tebboune has in the past accused the Gulf state of trying to meddle in Algeria's internal affairs.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Aid cuts force clinic closures across Sudan</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d6e5e77d1a4ed73c56c3287c89e79fd1c3655baf2191ec0981793e6a4c0a90fc</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:54:01Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T20:32:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan--1bc1aa71732f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: aid groups are warning that funding cuts are forcing dozens of health clinics in Sudan to close. Also, an AI and Africa conference has kicked off in South Korea's capital. Finally, we take a look at the Rwanda Space Agency as Paris hosts its very first international space summit.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>JNIM jihadists step up incursions into northern Benin</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8cb7c2803851408c08a106650d367d9be0d4881198f5080c3113f75f24e4922b</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T10:00:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin--c61528c3ec0c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin" /><summary type="html">Al Qaeda affiliate JNIM has been intensifying its activities in Benin since 2022. The country serves as a transit corridor between the areas of operation of the group, which is well established in Mali, Niger and Burkina Faso. Two nature reserves have become logistical hubs for the jihadist fighters. Benin's army is striving to hold the line.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>Kenya cracks down on foreign traders</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cddc82fd6982e20aeb251a05a222b2134e46372ea5a7500855e77c9133d96b0b</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T20:29:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders--c31b8c5d613f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, Hundreds of Burundians flocked to ⁠their embassy in ​Nairobi to get travel documents to return home. Also, in his first speech to the National assembly, Senegal’s Prime Minister Ahmadou Al Aminou Lo slams the rise of xenophobic rhetoric on the continent. Also, FRANCE 24's Georja Calvin Smith speaks to the founder of an AI surgical non-profit about the work being done in Tanzania to extend the reach of the country's 27 overburdened neurosurgeons.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian influencer faces death penalty over drug charges</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1cf797285b00ea8e50cc64249629787c2a1d3e29d652a71b72839d47ab9d275</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T20:27:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges--5338612a10b4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: An Egyptian TV presenter is among a dozen people a court has sentenced to death on drug charges. But first: a South African high court rejects a former defence minister's bid to have a corruption case against her dropped. Plus the very first Pan-African Architecture Biennale opened its doors on Monday in the Kenyan capital Nairobi.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisians tell the story of their summer of shortages</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37fe91ed81452a0eb4daf7b9cebd8e5a0a0c4841c7a3e7b261016d08698f6529</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T18:50:16Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages--617d7548111f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages" /><summary type="html">Frequent water and power cuts, long queues for bottled water, poultry farms destroyed by the heat: these have been the scenes across Tunisia this summer as millions of people were affected by shortages in electricity and water. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment in infrastructure while the Tunisian president, for his part, blames "deliberate and planned acts of sabotage”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian TV presenter Sarah Khalifa sentenced to death in drug case</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0ae6eae6f844be900e188320cba69903cd3490b2c0c72300e2a49b9aae7ea883</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T13:52:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case--a5ffad89f8f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case" /><summary type="html">Sarah Khalifa, a well-known Egyptian TV presenter, has been sentenced to death for her involvement in a drug ring, state media reported over the weekend. Eleven others were also convicted in the case and handed a death sentence. Khalifa is best known for her program “Mission Impossible”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Uber withdraws from Nigeria and Uganda</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5555dbad04c002b987b88a0c2c5d9e5ab8c1e9b93b9510cb82b94c2fab639e23</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T20:39:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria--7fddf4430022.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria" /><summary type="html">In tonight's programme: Uber is pulling out of Nigeria and Uganda, leaving thousands of drivers scrambling for new sources of income. But first: a UN report says foreign fighters and support networks are fuelling Sudan's war and worsening violence against civilians. Finally, we discuss the African Diaspora International Film Festival with co-director Diarah N'daw-Spech and Laurence Cole, co-producer of the Nigerian film "Shadow of Greed".</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Moroccans are desperate: Years of political crackdown and lack of economic opportunities'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ce6435a13a2cb73205d027ddfbbc71e9b540da596353ce8d11ce9e617a558c88</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T12:30:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities--0197ef1ebe1f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities" /><summary type="html">François Picard is pleased to welcome Aida Alami, New York Times World contributor. Drawing on her own field reporting at the Moroccan border, Alami challenges attempts to explain the mass crossing to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta primarily through a theory of deliberate state orchestration. While acknowledging both Morocco's history of leveraging migration in its relations with Spain and the possibility that security forces made tactical decisions on the day, Alami insists on distinguishing evidence from inference.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Mass burial reveals hidden toll of Algeria’s deadly ‘Black Wednesday’ wildfires</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f4dee0e90303c601b4f0d4e24a9afd16cfdb6b83a1c43cbfe59e80abee8c1dd7</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T11:33:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires--e2ca45613885.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires" /><summary type="html">Wildfires ripped across northeastern Algeria in late August. Photos and videos showing the mass burial of several dozen people have raised questions about the real death toll of the fires as Algerian authorities haven’t shared new numbers since August 26. A number of Moroccan social media accounts have also been sharing disinformation about the fires.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Pragmatic international relations': Sánchez seeks to avoid 'further conflict with Rabat'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4d5afa2a8d2fbd2ea3e2529c6fd82282726506b1f618d07e8e25bd67395ecf97</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T05:22:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260904-pragmatic-international-relations-s_nchez-seeks-to-avoid-further-conflict-with-rabat--c4a5b9cb15c4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260904-pragmatic-international-relations-s%C3%A1nchez-seeks-to-avoid-further-conflict-with-rabat" /><summary type="html">Delano D'Souza is pleased to welcome Alana Moceri, International Relations Professor at IE School of Politics, Economics and Global Affairs. The migrant crossings at Ceuta have become both a domestic and international political lightning rod. At home, Pedro Sánchez faces opponents who can cast migration in existential terms, with Spain's far right amplifying the rhetoric of "invasion." Internationally, the crisis exposes the geopolitical leverage inherent in control of the border. Ceuta thus emerges as more than a migration crisis: the border becomes at once an instrument of geopolitical leverage, a weapon in Spain's domestic political contest, and a battleground for competing narratives and disinformation.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tunisia top court upholds long sentences in mass trial of opposition figures</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:607595261303c65bc016d27a375d74a2902cc4ebeb2c0fd04ce0eb9efbcc0916</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T22:17:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260903-tunisia-court-upholds-prison-sentences-of-up-to-45-years-for-opposition-figures--25780c534aec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260903-tunisia-court-upholds-prison-sentences-of-up-to-45-years-for-opposition-figures" /><summary type="html">Tunisia's top court on Thursday upheld prison sentences of up to 45 years for opposition leaders, lawyers and businessmen convicted in a major conspiracy case, in a ruling opponents said further entrenches President Kais Saied's authoritarian rule.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Shady surgeons and Nigeria organ harvesting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba28adc2084bfa6377f30c13bca9bab68ae47939922ec28147b6571ea33fbd4d</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T20:15:15Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/shady-surgeons-and-nigeria-organ-harvesting--d87a92aad99d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/shady-surgeons-and-nigeria-organ-harvesting" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Legal loopholes and gaps about specialised medicine may be driving illegal organ harvesting in Nigeria. Also, South Sudan may be moving closer to delivering on elections scheduled for December. Finally, on the islands of São Tomé and Príncipe, six colonial-era plantation estates have just been added to the UNESCO World Heritage List.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisia water crisis deepens as temperatures approach 50°C</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:11666dfbe8e9059765e60267d682f2f668cc16e443639c54ed1718a3093be443</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-03T13:52:23Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260903-tunisia-water-crisis-deepens-as-temperatures-approach-50_c--25ad983d7cc3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260903-tunisia-water-crisis-deepens-as-temperatures-approach-50%C2%B0c" /><summary type="html">In Tunisia, powerful heatwaves are driving power cuts and water shortage. While the country has been dealing with structural water scarcity and recurring drought for years, the current crisis is fuelling anger against the government, even as authorities attempt to coordinate water distribution efforts to quell the crisis.</summary></entry><entry><title>Kenyan editor found safe after abduction</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcfeacc90bcdf8035ccbe41bae25f96f99b45b0d1db0c4a853cc44e5aa2c17a3</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T20:15:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenyan-editor-found-safe-after-abduction--7cd7d68f36ec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/kenyan-editor-found-safe-after-abduction" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Media rights groups in Kenya call for an investigation after a top journalist is abducted overnight by heavily armed men. Also, trials for three potential vaccines against the Bundibugyo strain of Ebola are due to start over the next couple of months. Plus Senegalese consumers who are frustrated by the rising cost of living have launched a new campaign.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Spanish report flags Moroccan police's 'total permissiveness' in Ceuta migrant rush</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1b78abbb06a4251d2283a8237e3f87f3bbfa61c3d44baf5ad2e736daa47a788</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T19:20:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260902-spanish-report-flags-moroccan-police-s-total-permissiveness-in-ceuta-migrant-rush--02ef226c47f6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260902-spanish-report-flags-moroccan-police-s-total-permissiveness-in-ceuta-migrant-rush" /><summary type="html">Moroccan security forces displayed "total permissiveness" during last month's massive wave of migrants who crossed into the Spanish North African territory of Ceuta, according to a Spanish police report seen by a French news agency on Wednesday.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>IMF reaches historical loan agreement with Senegal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:71b9f3ed7793a6b88c701b14afff3568d74fd88702b119a4d626199c699df1d2</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T12:51:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/imf-reaches-historical-loan-agreement-with-senegal--2eb64631acbe.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/imf-reaches-historical-loan-agreement-with-senegal" /><summary type="html">The IMF and Senegal have agreed on a new 2.2 billion-dollar loan programme to support financial reforms, a relief for the country after a huge amount of unreported debt recently came to light. The president has accused his predecessor of hiding the true state of the country's imbalanced budget deficit.</summary></entry><entry><title>Kenya shifts to electric cars as fuel prices soar 50%</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8167e054380aea2acab39729415349ed639cf68033fef335498ae665035b43c2</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T11:19:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-kenya-shifts-to-electric-cars-as-fuel-prices-soar-50--214cc24f9987.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-kenya-shifts-to-electric-cars-as-fuel-prices-soar-50" /><summary type="html">In Kenya, locally made eletric vehicles are gaining traction as fuel prices rise. As more and more Kenyans consider going electric, local firms are stepping up to assemble EV's domestically.</summary></entry><entry><title>Illegal mining ravages Johannesburg neighbourhoods</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:641a47ed265d53dfdef5b4b13c3adf9331b6f11f4434bb38e751a759ca098dfa</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T11:14:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-illegal-mining-ravages-johannesburg-neighbourhoods--a75e47943773.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-illegal-mining-ravages-johannesburg-neighbourhoods" /><summary type="html">Some houses in one Johannesburg neighbourhood are in danger of collapse because of illegal mining. Known as Zama Zamas, around 34,000 illegal miners are thought to be working in South Africa, in the hope of finding gold and chromium. The mines they dig hollow out the ground, which causes buildings to collapse as well as frequent power outages.</summary></entry><entry><title>Military buildup seen in Ethiopia near Sudanese border</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:47abcaa39598f4e99d0611e4d7b272588622deb7c72b438b3058c1b55d32b3b5</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-02T10:57:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-military-buildup-seen-in-ethiopia-near-sudanese-border--3f84da8e9b1a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260902-military-buildup-seen-in-ethiopia-near-sudanese-border" /><summary type="html">A major military buildup has been observed on the Ethiopian side of the Sudanese border. It comes amid intensified fighting between the Sudanese armed forces and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF). Sudan and Ethiopia have both accused each other of supporting rebels within their borders.</summary></entry><entry><title>South Africans missing in Nepal's glacial flood</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a5188de0934d2ca64aa00c88f7bb4645e91f31d68343d10a07fb651f09819736</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T20:35:30Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/south-africans-missing-in-nepal-s-glacial-flood--856bd6e9f51b.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/south-africans-missing-in-nepal-s-glacial-flood" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Africa Disaster Relief is looking for six South Africans who went missing in the deadly floods on the border between Nepal and China. Also, South African illegal miners are undermining their own communities. Finally, rising fuel prices are pushing Kenyans towards electric vehicles.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Niger's thwarted mutiny highlights junta's dependence on Russia</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:de624b5e4d0b23aed4905df76de622fbe37eec08740daa0b1c7fecd66ac1507e</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T15:16:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260901-what-russia-helping-niger-junta-stop-mutiny-means-for-its-sovereignty--ec75ac36d492.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260901-what-russia-helping-niger-junta-stop-mutiny-means-for-its-sovereignty" /><summary type="html">A failed mutiny in Niger over the weekend shows how important Russia's Africa Corps mercenaries are in the country, according to analysts. They argue that the failed mutiny on Saturday shows how the country's security landscape has worsened and contradicts claims from Niger's junta about the country's independence from foreign powers.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Ghana cocoa farmers reject new 'punitive' land conversion law</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:76ccc67a45d1fbd7cd594d8c54a2cf3a1a96895512128d9399bec82fea13e5a8</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T14:05:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-ghana-cocoa-farmers-reject-new-punitive-land-conversion-law--206a8b9097ca.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-ghana-cocoa-farmers-reject-new-punitive-land-conversion-law" /><summary type="html">Ghanaian cocoa farmers are ramping up their pushback against a new law preventing them from using their land for anything else without permission. President Mahama says supporting local processing is the key to economic growth. Cocoa chiefs say that the changes still leave the sector resting on an outdated colonial model.</summary></entry><entry><title>DR Congo's opposition rejects president's dialogue offer and calls for demonstration</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:da79fc5ef5c799eb9adf876e471eaf253cc1f018a023f365d148182baca3d57d</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T13:40:25Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-dr-congo-s-opposition-rejects-president-s-dialogue-offer-and-calls-for-demonstration--b2e303a5a5e8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-dr-congo-s-opposition-rejects-president-s-dialogue-offer-and-calls-for-demonstration" /><summary type="html">In DR Congo, managing the spread of Ebola is made all the more difficult as the country is juggling simultaneous security crises including untangling a peace deal with M23 rebels. President Felix Tshisekedi offered to convene a national dialogue which opposition parties call a smoke screen hiding an attempted power grab.</summary></entry><entry><title>As capital goes back to normal, Niger downplays soldiers' mutiny</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ef539a0ccbb0109a914c6af0484bcf17d8757ed9f0111dab5c52c1459db7c48d</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T10:51:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-as-capital-goes-back-to-normal-niger-downplays-soldiers-mutiny--e59d539f0a3d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260901-as-capital-goes-back-to-normal-niger-downplays-soldiers-mutiny" /><summary type="html">Niger's capital is back to normal after an intense week-end. A soldier mutiny took over Niamey on August 29, which was stopped by the army alongside the Russian paramilitary Africa Corps. The next day, Algeria sent four fighters aircrafts and two other military planes as cooperation with Nigeria is recently back on : an unexpected decision. Since it got independence, Algeria's non intervention doctrine is part of its DNA. FRANCE 24's Harold Girard tells us more about the goal of this decision.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tunisia's iconic Sidi Bou Said town added to UNESCO world heritage list</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d968522897eb01b8115de9d21df4691344efe24c0962eb376987bb05d9ab76f0</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-09-01T10:07:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tunisia-s-iconic-sidi-bou-said-town-added-to-unesco-world-heritage-list--114022c5711c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tunisia-s-iconic-sidi-bou-said-town-added-to-unesco-world-heritage-list" /><summary type="html">In Tunisia, UNESCO recognised the town of Sidi Bou Said as a world heritage site this July. The designation marks a new period in the cultural preservation of this historic tourist site. The special status may help as it battles erosion, like in the floods this last January, but it will also help boost its lesser known Sufi mystical traditions. FRANCE 24's Lilia Blaise and Hamdi Tlili were there to report on the Kharja ceremonies, which run in the town from mid-august til the end of September.</summary></entry><entry><title>Kenyan airport staff strike delays thousands of passengers</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ebc12222f3de8463d2789625f3439c10ca6e9282d2e8d6e1824dbc9731ec26d1</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-08-31T20:20:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenya-airport-staff-strike-delays-thousands-of-passengers--de2fae5b8da6.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/kenya-airport-staff-strike-delays-thousands-of-passengers" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: A workers' strike at Kenya's main airport leaves travellers grounded. Also, Ghanaian cocoa farmers are ramping up their pushback against a new law preventing them from using their land for anything else without permission. And in Tunisia, the picturesque town of Sidi Bou Saïd has been recognised by UNESCO as being important to global heritage.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Niger junta puts down mutiny with help from Russia's Africa Corps</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:36f63138f07cdc286f0eaecf94736c2603103fcf6429712e7db19c2026376fb1</id><updated>2026-09-11T04:51:55Z</updated><published>2026-08-30T15:20:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260830-niger-junta-aided-by-russian-paramilitary-forces-regains-control-after-mutiny--b57d1305a2a5.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260830-niger-junta-aided-by-russian-paramilitary-forces-regains-control-after-mutiny" /><summary type="html">Life returned to normal in Niger's capital on Sunday, a day after mutineers stormed both the presidential palace and a military base at Niamey's main airport, resisting forces loyal to the ruling junta until Russia’s Africa Corps intervened. State TV reported that dozens of soldiers were killed or arrested, with some paraded in front of cameras live on television.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry></feed>
